TRAC MPIAE Changes
===================


Current WIP -- Version 0.23.0 -- Make instructions title configurable
--------------------------------------------------------
- FIX: the backend now ships `psycopg` (v3) as its PostgreSQL driver instead of
  `psycopg2-binary`, and declares an explicit `sqlalchemy>=2.1` floor. SQLAlchemy
  2.1 changed the driver it loads for a plain `postgresql://` URL from psycopg2 to
  psycopg, so a fresh install of the wheel (as the wheel smoke test does, resolving
  dependencies unpinned) picked up SQLAlchemy 2.1.4 and then died on
  `ModuleNotFoundError: No module named 'psycopg'`. SQLModel had to move to 0.0.48
  (its versions below that cap SQLAlchemy to < 2.1) and uv.lock was re-resolved for
  sqlalchemy 2.1.4, sqlmodel 0.0.48, psycopg 3.3.6. No configuration changes are
  needed: every `postgresql://` URL (env templates, CI, Docker stacks) keeps working.
- NEW: make page title of instructions text configurable by user via study_text_instructions_title key in studies_config.json section of study.
- FIX: record the stored (DBMS-normalized) capture instant as the browser
  identification blob's `first_captured_at` instead of the pre-write Python
  timestamp. MySQL/MariaDB and SQL Server keep less sub-second precision than
  PostgreSQL, so the datetime column and the blob used to disagree on the same
  capture, which made the backend integration tests fail on those DBMS.
- FIX: a submitted study can no longer be edited through the API. The activity
  submission and consent endpoints now return HTTP 403 once the participant's
  `study_submitted_at` is set; previously only the frontend redirected submitted
  participants to the thank-you page, so the endpoints still accepted diary and
  consent changes when called directly. External task confirmations intentionally
  remain possible after submission.
- FIX: the consent page no longer reports permanent failures as temporary ones. A
  broken or expired participant link (400/403 `study_access_denied`), a paused or
  closed study (403 `study_unavailable`) and an already submitted study (403 on
  the consent POST) each get their own message now; before, every one of them
  ended in "please try again" / "reload the page", which could never succeed.
- FIX: the consent page retries transient failures (network error, 5xx) twice with
  linear backoff, like the diary's config sync, so a short backend restart during
  maintenance no longer shows an error at all. When the consent information cannot
  be loaded the accept/decline buttons now stay disabled - the page used to offer
  them anyway, so a participant could consent to text that never appeared (the
  "could not load / could not save" report from the MPIAE pilot study).
- DEV: the retry policy now lives in `frontend/src/js/api_request.js` and is
  shared by the diary config sync and the consent page; both the retry policy and
  the failure classification have unit tests, and the consent error states have
  Playwright coverage.
- NEW: activity blocks on desktop show a small grip on their left and right edge
  (hover, keyboard focus, or while resizing). The edges could always be dragged to
  adjust a duration, but nothing on the block said so, and participants asked for
  a visible marker in the style of an Excel fill handle. The grips are decoration
  (`pointer-events: none`), so the resize zones and cursors are unchanged; they
  are not shown on the phone layout (deferred, see `dev_tools/todo_mobile.md`).
- FIX: on a phone a swipe that starts on an activity block scrolls the day again.
  `touch-action: none` on `.activity-block` turned the block surface - about 75 %
  of the timeline width - into a scroll dead zone, reported as "the scrolling
  doesn't work on my phone"; measured with real touch input it moved the timeline
  0 px, while the same swipe 30 px further left scrolled it. The block body may
  pan vertically now, and the top/bottom bands keep `touch-action: none`
  (`activity-block-edge-zone`) so a resize is not cancelled by a scroll mid-drag.
- FIX: a timeline with no coverage requirement is no longer reported as
  "requirement met" while it is empty. `min_coverage: 0` is satisfied by nothing
  at all, so the secondary timeline of the pilot study showed a green ✓ next to
  its name before the participant had entered anything - read as "this one is
  done, skip it". A ✓ is now only shown where the study requires something and
  that requirement is met; no requirement plus no entries reads as "nothing
  entered yet" in a neutral colour, and the red shortfall text is unchanged. The
  timeline menu shows the same wording as the timeline itself (it had the same
  vacuous ✓), both from one formatter in `frontend/src/js/ui.js`.
- NEW: on a phone an untouched second timeline is advertised inside the day it
  belongs to. Below the breakpoint only the active timeline is laid out (every
  other container measures 0×0), so nothing on the page mentioned the other
  timeline and a participant who never opened the context bar's timeline picker
  never learned it existed. The active timeline's label block now lists one
  tappable row per other timeline that is still empty on the current day
  ("Secondary Activity · nothing entered yet ›"); a tap switches timelines
  through the same path the picker uses, and the row disappears as soon as that
  timeline has an entry. Desktop shows both timelines side by side and gets no
  rows.
- NEW: every place a timeline names itself now also says where it sits in the
  set - "Main Activity (1/2)": the timeline title, the phone context bar, the
  timeline menu and the desktop switch-timeline button. Same compact pattern the
  phone day counter already uses ("(3/7)"), and plain numbers, so it needed no
  translation. A study with a single timeline is unchanged ("(1/1)" would
  announce a choice that does not exist).

Version 0.22.0 -- Optional opt-in browser identification
--------------------------------------------------------
- NEW: browser/device identification, so researchers can tell whether the app
  works for participants on different devices (it complements the questions
  participants are asked about their experience). A study opts in with the new
  `save_browser_identification` setting. For this, `ua-parser-js` is vendored
  in `frontend/src/assets/ua-parser-js/` at version 1.0.41, the last
  MIT-licensed release.
- DEV: migration 0011 adds `studies.save_browser_identification` and
  `study_participants.user_agent` / `client_info` /
  `client_info_captured_at`.
- DEV: `README_CREATE_STUDY.md` documents the setting as opt-in and points out
  that the data is personal and usage of the feature belongs in the consent text,
  and `README.md` documents the new participant export alongside the activities export.



Version 0.21.0 -- New mobile interface, i18n hardening, clarify DB migration policy
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
- CHG: drop the duplicate "Save Day" button from the diary footer. Both it and
  the button in the toolbar above the timeline called the very same save
  routine, and the toolbar one is the one users reach (it is sticky on phones).
  The footer now collapses entirely while it has no visible action, which is the
  case for every study with `allow_skip_timeuse: false`: an empty action row was
  costing a full row of vertical space on phones.
- NEW: the database schema is documented as forward-only, and that policy now has
  tests. `backend/tests/migrations/` (run with the new `./test_backend_migrations.sh`,
  which starts a throwaway database) executes the real migrations, which no other
  suite did - the integration tests build their schema with
  `SQLModel.metadata.create_all()` and so never run them:
  * `upgrade head` on an empty database, and upgrading twice,
  * upgrading a database that already holds data from *every* revision to head and
    then running `tud studies import` against it (the deployment that actually
    happens, and the only place 0004's data conversion runs),
  * adopting a database created by `create_all()` before Alembic existed via
    `stamp` + `upgrade`,
  * `alembic check`: a change in `models.py` without a migration now fails CI.
  There is deliberately no downgrade test: reverting a migration that dropped a
  column cannot restore the data, and 0004 cannot reverse its type change at all.
- FIX: adopting a database that was created before Alembic existed failed on
  MariaDB. Migration 0004 changed `studies.description` to JSON before rewriting
  its values, but MariaDB validates the rows that are already in the table when
  the column becomes JSON, so a legacy plain-text description aborted the whole
  upgrade (`CONSTRAINT studies.description failed`). The values are now made
  JSON-safe first - `JSON_VALID()` keeps objects and arrays, `JSON_QUOTE()` turns
  plain text into a JSON string - and the column type is changed afterwards.
  Found by the new migration tests, which now also run on MariaDB.
- NEW: `.github/workflows/migrations.yml` runs those tests on every push and pull
  request (PostgreSQL), and on MariaDB/MSSQL on demand
  (`gh workflow run migrations.yml -f run_dbms_matrix=true`) - their migration
  branches (0004's type conversion, 0010's constraint/index handling) had never
  been executed on those DBMSes.
- DEV: `README.md` ("Database schema policy"), `docs/agents/database.md` and the
  database skill describe the forward-only policy: migrations stay additive for the
  deployed code (nullable/server defaults, data moves in its own step, columns are
  dropped a release after they stop being used), undoing a release means redeploying
  the previous release or restoring a backup, and a pre-Alembic database is adopted
  once with `alembic stamp`.
- DEV: `backend/alembic.ini` declares `path_separator = os` (Alembic >= 1.11), which
  removes the deprecation warning about splitting `prepend_sys_path`.
- FIX (CI, surfaced by the first run with the new guards): the WebKit leg
failed because the activity info dialog carried `role="dialog"` on its inner
wrapper while only the overlay around it is shown and hidden. `js/ui.js`
detects open/close by watching the style of the `[role="dialog"]` element, so
that dialog counted as permanently open: focus was never given back, and WebKit
keeps focus on a control that just became invisible (Chromium moves it out by
itself, which is why only WebKit complained). The role/aria attributes now sit
on the overlay like every other dialog, the visibility check additionally
requires a layout box, and focus is blurred when the element that opened the
dialog cannot take it (the body is not focusable).
- FIX (CI): `frontend/tests/e2e/mobile_layout.spec.js` failed on WebKit because
the phone timeline is a tall column inside a clipped pane and the legal footer
is painted over the part that overflows it - tapping "the middle of the
timeline, clamped to the viewport" landed on a footer link, which no browser
routes to the timeline. Both specs now ask the new `findTimelinePoint()` helper
in `e2e_helpers.js` for a point the timeline actually receives.
- DEV: the e2e workflow no longer marks the `Europe/Berlin` leg as failed after
a green test run - the artifact name contained the matrix timezone and artifact
names may not contain a forward slash, so the upload step (not the tests) failed;
the test step now exports a slash-free `TZ_LABEL` and the uploads use it.
- DEV: `no_broken_static_requests.spec.js` ignores requests the browser itself
cancelled (Firefox and WebKit abort font downloads they stop needing; a missing
file answers 404, which the response handler still catches) and waits for
`document.fonts.ready` before asserting, so the guard is not timing-dependent.
- FIX (i18n text): the thank-you page's built-in message named the app, so it
read as "...Ihre Teilnahme an unserer Studie TRAC" - as if the participant had
signed up for TRAC instead of for the study. This is a leftover from when the app
was called "TimeUse" and the sentence was built around the app name; the app name
is now gone from `thankYou.defaultMessage` in every locale (en, de, es, fi, fr,
pl, sv) and from the static fallback in `frontend/src/pages/thank-you.html` (the
`.app-name` rule went with it). Study-specific end texts come from the study
config and are unaffected - they are simply shown instead of the default.
- FIX (accessibility, found by the new axe check): the diary's "Skip time
reporting" button rendered white text on its light grey background (1.23:1 - the
label was effectively invisible) because a later `.btn { color: white }` rule
overrode `.skip-btn`; the legal-footer links were #ccc on the page background
(1.53:1) and so were unreadable on the German pages; the timeline helper texts
and the inactive-timeline title were grey on grey (3.28:1 / 2.68:1); the idle
timer readout was just under the limit (4.09:1); the timeline containers carried
an `aria-label` without a role (assistive tech drops it); and the viewport meta
blocked pinch-zoom (`user-scalable=no`).
- FIX (phone layout): pages that do not load the shared stylesheet combined
`width: 100%` with `box-sizing: content-box`, so on a 390px viewport the open
studies page ran 77px wide, tasks 26px, timeout 22px and the German instructions
page 58px - `overflow-x: hidden` then cut the content off instead of showing it.
The diary timeline ran 4px wide (nested `width: 100%` + padding), and the German
legal-footer row could not wrap at all (inline links with no break opportunity),
so "Impressum - Datenschutz - Studieninformation" was clipped; the footer links
are also 24px tall now instead of 16px (WCAG 2.2 SC 2.5.8).
- DEV: new accessibility guard `frontend/tests/e2e/accessibility.spec.js` runs
axe-core (WCAG 2.0/2.1/2.2 A + AA) over every participant page plus the activity
picker and activity info dialogs, and fails on any violation; `@axe-core/playwright`
is a dev dependency. Verified to fail when the zoom-blocking meta is restored.
- DEV: new `frontend/tests/e2e/keyboard_accessibility.spec.js` covers the dialog
focus management in `js/ui.js` (focus moves into the opened dialog, Tab and
Shift+Tab cannot leave it, Escape closes it and returns focus) and the timeline
containers (named `role="group"`, focusable); `frontend/tests/e2e/mobile_layout.spec.js`
checks that no participant page scrolls sideways on a phone, that the main diary
controls are at least 24x24 and that an activity can be placed with touch events
alone. All three were verified to fail when the fixed behaviour is reverted.
- DEV: `frontend/tests/e2e/participant_pages.js` now holds the list of
participant pages, so the static-asset, accessibility and mobile guards sweep the
same set (this added the tasks page to the static-request check).
- FIX: the diary's overlap validation (used by the drag/resize/undo handlers, which
revert an invalid change) compared `new Date("07:30")` - an Invalid Date in every
engine - so it could never fire and invalid drags were kept. It now compares the
absolute minutes-of-day via `getActivityTimeRangeMinutes()`; `Timeline.validate()`
in `frontend/src/js/timeline.js` is covered by
`frontend/tests/unit/timeline_validation.test.js`.
- DEV: removed the unused `formatTimeDDMMYYYYHHMM()` helper, which derived real
calendar dates from the browser's local "today" (activities carry minutes-of-day,
not dates).
- DEV: the E2E workflow runs a second chromium leg with `TZ=Europe/Berlin` so
timezone/DST assumptions surface in CI instead of on a participant's phone.
- FIX: the diary's unsaved work is now kept via `frontend/src/js/draft_storage.js`,
which never throws when storage is unavailable or full (Safari private mode,
"block all cookies", quota exceeded - one `localStorage.setItem` on the
instruction banner was unguarded) and discards stored drafts that are corrupt,
structurally unusable or written by an older app version instead of feeding them
to the timeline. The participant/study/day check now lives in
`matchesTimelineContext()`, covered by unit tests.
- DEV: new `frontend/tests/unit/draft_storage.test.js` (quota, blocked storage,
corrupt JSON, unusable payloads, freshness window, other-participant draft,
clearing) and E2E `frontend/tests/e2e/draft_restore.spec.js` asserting that
unsaved activities survive a reload (verified to fail when the restore is
disabled).
- DEV: new static-asset guards: `frontend/tests/unit/static_assets.test.js`
checks that every asset referenced by HTML/CSS exists, and the new E2E spec
`frontend/tests/e2e/no_broken_static_requests.spec.js` visits all participant
pages and fails on any static request that does not succeed (missing GIF,
locale, settings file, font) - the class of bug that only the deployment used to
notice.
- DEV: `frontend/tests/unit/locale_values.test.js` guards translation *values*:
`{{placeholders}}` and the HTML tags of every key must match the reference
locale, so a translator cannot drop `{{days}}` or break `data-i18n-html` markup
without the suite complaining.
- FIX: the admin "study not found" page reflected the study name from the URL
path unescaped (`/admin/study/<img src=x onerror=...>` executed script in the
administrator's authenticated browser). Values are escaped now; new guards:
`tests/unit/test_html_escaping_guards.py` (no f-string HTML in HTMLResponse, no
`|safe` in admin templates) and `tests/integration/test_admin_html_escaping.py`
(reflected URL value + stored study name are rendered escaped).
- FIX: half-translated pages (e.g. German "Tagebuch absenden" heading above an
English body on the instructions page) were caused by a stale browser-cached
locale file: i18n.t() keeps the built-in English markup text for keys it cannot
resolve, so an old de.json mixes languages once a page uses new keys.
- FIX: js/i18n.js now revalidates the locale file (fetch cache: 'no-cache') and,
if keys were still missing, re-fetches it once with a cache-buster and applies
the fresh copy before reporting the remaining keys as a real translation gap.
- NEW: I18n.tOptional()/has() for optional keys (deployment-overridable labels
like footer.imprint) so expected misses no longer log warnings or trigger a
locale re-fetch; footer.js uses them.
- NEW: pages/timeout.html initialised no i18n at all (title/heading/message were
English for everyone) and the inactivity redirect passed no language; both are
fixed (timeout.title/heading/message, language appended to the redirect URL).
- FIX: missing i18n wiring on other pages: consent language label + select
aria-label, consent tooltips/review prompt/load error, consent page title per
study, thank-you default outro text, "Access study '<name>'" and the error text
on the open-studies page, tasks page title, the Submit Study tooltip and the
document title on the diary page.
- FIX: the instructions page "Loading..." GIF placeholder was hardcoded in
instructions.css; it is a real translated element now (common.loading). The GIF
alt texts are translated too (new data-i18n-alt support in i18n.js).
- FIX: messages.copyFromDay was referenced by script.js/ui.js but missing from
all 7 locale files, so the "Copy from..." button showed the raw key.
- DEV: new frontend/tests/unit/page_i18n_keys.test.js asserts that every key
requested by markup/scripts exists in every locale and that no stylesheet
contains hardcoded user-visible text; new i18n_missing_keys.test.js covers the
revalidation/repair/optional-lookup behaviour.


